Unofficial cloud integrations
-----------------------------

The Toshiba and Petkit plugins reach the vendors' cloud services through
interfaces that the vendors do not document or offer to third parties. They work
the way each vendor's own phone app does. Using them may be against the vendor's
terms of service, and they can stop working whenever the vendor changes
something. They are marked "unofficial" in the app, and each one's setup begins
with this warning. Use them at your own risk.


Where the protocol knowledge came from
--------------------------------------

Several plugins speak protocols that their vendors do not publish. What they
know about those protocols was learned from the open-source projects and public
documents below. In each case only facts about the protocol were used (message
layouts, field names, codes, paths and the values a server accepts). No source
code, and no table or test data in its original form, was copied from any of
them into Swifty Home. Each plugin's DEVELOPING.md lists the sources in detail.

  toshiba-ac (Toshiba-AC-control), by Kamil Sroka and contributors
    https://github.com/KaSroka/Toshiba-AC-control
    Licence: Apache License 2.0.
    Used by the Toshiba plugin: the cloud's sign-in, unit list and state paths,
    the device registration, the layout of the compact unit state, the mode and
    fan codes, and the shape of the command message.

  pypetkitapi (py-petkit-api), by Jezza34000 and contributors
    https://github.com/Jezza34000/py-petkit-api
    Licence: MIT License.
    Used by the Petkit plugin: the sign-in, the regional servers, the headers
    and client description the Android app sends (whose values are the plugin's
    defaults), and the feeder and litter box endpoints and their fields.

  The Home Assistant Petkit integration (homeassistant_petkit), by Jezza34000
  and contributors
    https://github.com/Jezza34000/homeassistant_petkit
    Licence: MIT License.
    Used by the Petkit plugin: how the day's records give the feed count, the
    litter box's working state and a finished cleaning.

  tinytuya, by Jason Cox and contributors
    https://github.com/jasonacox/tinytuya
    Licence: MIT License.
  tuyapi, by Max Isom and contributors
    https://github.com/codetheweb/tuyapi
    Licence: MIT License.
    Used by the Tuya plugin: the frame layout, encryption and commands of the
    local protocol 3.3. The plugin's cloud lookup follows Tuya's own public
    developer documentation for cloud projects.

  pywizlight, by Stephan Traub and contributors
    https://github.com/sbidy/pywizlight
    Licence: MIT License.
    Used by the WiZ plugin: the local UDP protocol on port 38899 (getPilot,
    setPilot and their fields).

  The LG webOS client libraries lgtv2 and PyWebOSTV
    https://github.com/hobbyquaker/lgtv2 (Sebastian Raff and contributors)
    https://github.com/supersaiyanmode/PyWebOSTV (Srivatsan Iyer and contributors)
    Licence: MIT License, both.
    Used by the webOS plugin: the SSAP pairing message and its permission list,
    the ssap:// addresses for power, inputs and the foreground app, and the
    pointer socket for remote keys. Wake-on-LAN follows AMD's "Magic Packet
    Technology" white paper.

  SoCo, by Rahim Sonawalla and contributors
    https://github.com/SoCo/SoCo
    Licence: MIT License.
    Used by the Sonos plugin, beside the UPnP Forum's published UPnP AV
    specifications: the Sonos extensions to them (night mode through GetEQ and
    SetEQ, and the stream addresses of the TV and line inputs).

  Signify's Hue developer documentation (developers.meethue.com)
    The Hue plugin follows the documented Hue Bridge REST API, version 1:
    pairing with the link button, lights, groups, and the brightness and colour
    scales. Signify publishes this documentation for developers who build
    products that work with Hue; no text from it is reproduced here.

The other plugins use only documented interfaces: Moonraker's web API and
Klipper's status reference (Klipper and Moonraker are GPL-3.0 software; no code
from them is included), the Proxmox VE API (Proxmox VE is AGPL-3.0 software by
Proxmox Server Solutions GmbH; no code from it is included), Plex's support
documentation on the X-Plex-Token header, RFC 2326 (RTSP) for the camera probe,
and Microsoft's Azure IoT Hub documentation for sending a device-to-cloud
message over HTTPS. The Glimdock plugin speaks Glimdock's own HTTP contract.

The VNC console in the web app is a small client written for this product from
the RFB protocol (RFC 6143). Its DES routine, needed only for the VNC handshake,
is written from the DES standard (FIPS 46-3) and its published tables. It is
not adapted from noVNC or any other VNC client.


